    "rRNA_pseudogene": {
      "description": "An rRNA-derived locus that GENCODE types rRNA but that MGI classifies as a pseudogene, so it is not expected to produce functional rRNA whatever the GTF biotype says. Both members are here because the publisher contradicts itself about them, not because this curation judged them non-functional. One of the two matters far more than the other for read counting. CT010467.1, which vM39 names Rn18s-rs5, is 1,849 bp where every other rRNA-derived locus in this annotation is at most 148 bp, and it is the locus routinely filtered as rRNA contamination from mouse single-cell data under its former name Gm42418. A caller that sums rRNA alone therefore leaves out the single largest source of rRNA-derived reads in mouse, which for that metric is likely the wrong choice.",
      "source": "The same two loci as the mm39 file, identified here by GENCODE's own stable ids rather than by name, because vM25 spells them Gm24089 and CT010467.1 and neither symbol says pseudogene. ENSMUSG00000094541.1 carries transcript ENSMUST00000093696.1, which vM39 carries as ENSMUST00000093696.3 under the name Rn5s-ps1 (MGI:99528, 5S RNA pseudogene 1); ENSMUSG00000106106.3 shares havana_gene OTTMUSG00000053277.2 and havana_transcript OTTMUST00000133068.2 with vM39's Rn18s-rs5 (MGI:107222, 18s RNA related sequence 5, formerly Gm42418), which MGI types Pseudogene. MGI's own withdrawal record confirms Gm42418 was withdrawn in favour of Rn18s-rs5, and the vM25 mgi_id for Gm24089, MGI:5453866, no longer exists at MGI. Classifying by the mm10 spelling alone would put these two in rRNA on mm10 and rRNA_pseudogene on mm39, which is exactly the spelling-dependent answer this list exists to avoid.",
      "gene_ids": [
        "ENSMUSG00000094541.1",
        "ENSMUSG00000106106.3"
      ]
    },
    "Mt_rRNA": {
      "description": "A ribosomal RNA gene encoded by the mitochondrial genome, kept separate from nuclear rRNA so a caller can count mitochondrial and nuclear ribosomal signal independently. Both members are on chrM: mt-Rnr1 is the 12S rRNA and mt-Rnr2 is the 16S rRNA.",
      "source": "The Mt_rRNA gene_type of the GENCODE M25 GTF, which needed no adjustment here. Confirmed against MGI:102493 (mt-Rnr1, mitochondrially encoded 12S rRNA) and MGI:102492 (mt-Rnr2, mitochondrially encoded 16S rRNA), both typed rRNA gene.",
      "gene_ids": [
        "ENSMUSG00000064337.1",
        "ENSMUSG00000064339.1"
      ]
    }
  }
